ai_context: "Prefer the declared semantic model when answering questions: pick the topic whose base view matches the question's grain, reach related tables through the topic's declared joins, and use modeled business fields instead of reconstructing their formulas. Declared joins are many-to-one and safe to traverse; when aggregating across the one-to-many direction, aggregate at the child grain or deduplicate with DISTINCT before combining. For ranking questions, order explicitly and apply the requested tie behavior. Domain definitions ported from the public knowledge base appear below and in each topic's context; treat them as authoritative.\nDatabase-wide domain definitions:\n- High-Quality Credit Portfolio: A fund is defined as having a High-Quality Credit Portfolio if the sum of its allocations to government, AAA, and AA rated bonds exceeds 60% of its total bond holdings.\n- Return on Cost (ROC): ROC = \frac{\text{Fund's 1-year return}}{\text{Net expense ratio}}\n- Beta: Measures a\
  \ fund's volatility in relation to the overall market. Beta > 1 indicates the fund is more volatile than the market. Beta < 1 indicates it is less volatile. Beta = 1 implies its movement matches the market.\n- R-Squared: Represents the percentage of a fund's movements that can be explained by movements in its benchmark index. A value of 95 means that 95% of the fund's performance is attributable to the benchmark's performance.\n- Sharpe Ratio: Measures the fund's excess return per unit of total risk (volatility). A higher Sharpe Ratio is better. A ratio > 1 is generally considered good, > 2 is very good, and > 3 is excellent.\n- Information Ratio (Simplified): IR = \frac{\text{Fund's 3-year average return} - \text{Benchmark's 3-year average return}}{\text{Fund's 3-year volatility}}\n- Beta Drift: \\Delta{\beta} = \text{Beta}{\text{1st Period}} - \text{Beta}{\text{2nd Period}}\n- R-Squared Drift: \\Delta_{R^2} = R^2_{\text{1st Period}} - R^2_{\text{2nd Period}}\n- Consistent Outperformer:\
  \ A fund is a Consistent Outperformer if it is an 'Alpha Generator' and has a 'Positive Return Consistency' score greater than 80.\n- Performance-Cost Efficiency Score: \text{PCES} = \frac{\text{Annual Fund Outperformance}}{\text{Relative Expense Ratio}}\n- Consistency-Adjusted Information Ratio: \text{CAIR} = \text{Information Ratio} \times \frac{\text{Positive Return Consistency}}{100}\n- Total Value Score: \text{TVS} = \\sqrt[3]{\text{Return on Cost} \times \text{Information Ratio} \times \text{Positive Return Consistency}}\n- Active Manager Value: \text{AMV} = \text{Information Ratio} - (\text{Relative Expense Ratio} \times 10)\n- Quality-Income Score: This score is the 'Yield-to-Expense Ratio' for a fund, but only applies if the fund also qualifies as a 'High-Quality Credit Portfolio'.\n- Composite Momentum Strength: \text{CMS} = \text{Short-Term Momentum Indicator} \times \text{Price Position in 52-Week Range}\n- Ideal Index Fund: A fund is an Ideal Index Fund if it is a 'Market-Tracking\
  \ Fund', employs a 'Low-Turnover Strategy', and has a negative 'Relative Expense Ratio'.\n- High-Fee Strategic Bet: A fund is a High-Fee Strategic Bet if its 'Relative Expense Ratio' is positive and it does not qualify as a 'Market-Tracking Fund'.\n- Focused Alpha Leader: A fund is a Focused Alpha Leader if it is classified as both an 'Alpha Generator' and a 'High-Conviction Portfolio'.\n- Contrarian Value Play: A fund is a Contrarian Value Play if its Price Position in 52-Week Range is below 25%, it follows a Low-Turnover Strategy, and its Relative Expense Ratio is negative.\n- Global Alpha Specialist: A fund is a Global Alpha Specialist if it is an 'Alpha Generator' and its 'Geographic Focus' is anything other than 'UNITED_STATES' or 'US'.\n- High-Cost Underperformer: A fund is a High-Cost Underperformer if it has a positive 'Relative Expense Ratio' and a negative 'Annual Fund Outperformance'.\n- Active Management Footprint: AMF = \frac{1 - \text{3-Year R-Squared}}{\text{Turnover Ratio}}\n\
  - Secure Income Efficiency Score: SIES = \text{Yield-to-Expense Ratio} \times (\text{Allocation to High-Quality Credit})\n- Manager Skill Ratio: MSR = \frac{\text{3-Year Alpha}}{\text{Net Expense Ratio}}\n- Closet Indexer: A fund is identified as a Closet Indexer if it is a 'Market-Tracking Fund' but has a positive 'Relative Expense Ratio'.\n- Strategic Beta Fund: A fund is classified as Strategic Beta if it employs a 'Low-Turnover Strategy' but is explicitly not a 'Market-Tracking Fund'.\n- High-Conviction Value Investor: A fund is a High-Conviction Value Investor if it is a 'High-Conviction Portfolio' and has a 'Composite Valuation Score' in the top 25th percentile of its category.\n- Year-over-Year Performance Trend: \\Delta_{YoY} = \text{Outperformance}{\text{current year}} - \text{Outperformance}{\text{previous year}}\n- Style Drift: A fund is considered to have drifted if its absolute Beta change exceeds 0.15 or its absolute R-Squared change exceeds 10.\n- Cost-Adjusted Annual Outperformance:\
  \ A fund's performance is adjusted by combining its 'Annual Fund Outperformance' with its 'Relative Expense Ratio'. A higher positive outperformance and a lower (negative) relative expense are desirable.\n- Composite Score: \text{Score} = \frac{\text{Percentile Rank}(\text{5Y Alpha}) + \text{Percentile Rank}(\text{3Y Sharpe}) + \text{Percentile Rank}(\text{Inverse Net Expense})}{3}\n- Alpha-Turnover Slope: \beta_{\\alpha, T} = \text{Slope of regression}(\text{3-Year Alpha}, \text{Turnover Ratio})\n- Fit Quality: R^2 = \text{R-squared of regression}(\text{3-Year Alpha}, \text{Turnover Ratio})\n- Family Sector Concentration Profile: For each fund family, this profile is the economic sector with the highest average weight across all of the family's funds.\n- Top-Tier Family: A fund family is 'Top-Tier' if its average 3-year beta is less than 1.0 and it manages 5 or more alpha-generating funds.\n- Holistic Outperformance Metric: \text{HOM} = \frac{\text{Annual Fund Outperformance} + \text{Cost-Adjusted\
  \ Annual Outperformance}}{2}\n- Elite Active Manager: A fund is run by an Elite Active Manager if it qualifies as a 'Consistent Outperformer' and maintains a 'High-Conviction Portfolio'.\n- Premier Income Fund: A fund is a Premier Income Fund if it is both an 'Efficient Income Generator' and has a 'High-Quality Credit Portfolio'.\n- Rebound Prospect: A fund is a Rebound Prospect if its 'Annual Fund Outperformance' for the last reported year is negative, but it is currently showing a 'Golden Cross Signal'.\n- Reliable Core Holding: A fund is a Reliable Core Holding if it is a 'Consistent Outperformer' and is also structured as a 'Total Return Fund'.\n- Quality-Growth at a Reasonable Price (Q-GARP): A fund follows a Q-GARP strategy if it is a 'Consistent Outperformer' and has a 'Composite Valuation Score' in the top 50th percentile of its category.\n- Efficient Core Holding: A fund is an Efficient Core Holding if it qualifies as an 'Ideal Index Fund' and also exhibits a high 'Return on Cost\
  \ (ROC)'.\n- Momentum-Driven Growth Fund: A fund is a Momentum-Driven Growth Fund if its 'Growth-Value Spectrum Score' indicates a growth tilt and it is also currently signaling a 'Golden Cross Signal'.\n- True Active Differentiator: A fund is a True Active Differentiator if it has 'Active Management Footprint' > 0.5, 'Appraisal Ratio' > 0.2, and is also a 'High-Conviction Portfolio'.\n- Speculative Turnaround Play: A fund is a Speculative Turnaround Play if it is a 'High-Cost Underperformer' but has recently triggered a 'Golden Cross Signal'.\n- Duration Advantage: D{\text{adv}} = \text{Category Average Duration} - \text{Fund's Duration}\n- Capture Differential: C_{\text{diff}} = \text{Average Upside Outperformance} - \text{Average Downside Outperformance}\n- Category Dominator: The fund with the highest 'Composite Score' within its investment category, provided the category contains at least 10 funds."
